#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# function_singularities

#% text
# `function_singularities(f, x)` gives the condition on `x` at which `f` is singular: not defined, or not analytic.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The conditions are the negations of those of `function_domain`; at the boundary of a root's domain the function is singular too, so `sqrt(x)` is singular for `x <= 0`.

#% item
# `false` means no singularities.

#% item
# `function_singularities(f, x, complexes)` works over the complexes.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The poles of the tangent:

#% cell
function_singularities(tan(x), x)
#% output
#= cos(x) == 0

#% text
# A square root:

#% cell
function_singularities(sqrt(x), x)
#% output
#= x <= 0

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A rational function:

#% cell
function_singularities(1/(x^2 - 1), x)
#% output
#= -1 + x^2 == 0

#% text
# Several variables:

#% cell
function_singularities((x^2 + y^2)*csc(x*y), [x, y])
#% output
#= sin(x*y) == 0

#% text
# An entire function:

#% cell
function_singularities(exp(x) + sin(x), x)
#% output
#= false

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `function_domain` gives where the function is defined:

#% cell
function_domain(tan(x), x)
#% output
#= cos(x) != 0

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `function_domain`, `function_analytic`
